course: ai
lesson: 2
title: How a language model learns: guessing the next word
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A chatbot is built on a "large language model": large because it is enormous, language because it works with words, model because it is a copy of the patterns in how people write.
explain: It learned by playing a giant game of "guess the missing word" over and over with more text than you could read in thousands of lifetimes.
explain: Now, when you ask something, it builds an answer a little piece at a time, each time picking a word that fits well after the words before it.
explain: This is why it can say "I understand how you feel" in a kind voice: kind people often write that. Whether that counts as real understanding is something scientists still argue about, but it has never felt sad in a body.
explain: The answer is worked out on huge computers far away, in buildings called data centres; nobody is typing it.
fact: "Large language model" means a huge program that has learned the patterns of how words are used.
fact: A language model learned by predicting missing or next words in enormous amounts of text.
fact: It writes an answer piece by piece, each time choosing a likely next word.
fact: Its training text stops at a certain date, so a bot without a clock or web search may not know today's time or news.
fact: When the program is switched off, nothing happens inside it: it does not sleep, rest or dream.
fact: A bot has no body, so it is neither a boy nor a girl; a name and voice are design choices.
fact: Bots often give the most common human answer, so many of them pick 7 when asked for a number from 1 to 10.
fact: The text a bot learned from had good and bad parts; companies filter it and train the bot to be polite, but they do not catch everything.
fact: A bot is more than a parrot, because it can mix ideas in new ways, but everything it knows came from what people made.
fact: Picture-making AI learned from millions of pictures with descriptions and builds new pictures that fit the words.
fact: Chatbots run on giant computers in data centres; the words appearing one by one is just how the answer is shown.
exercise: Mia's bot says "I understand how you feel." Where does that sentence come from? | Kind people often write it, so the bot learned to say it | The bot has felt sad itself many times and remembers how it was | A worker read Mia's message and typed it | The bot can see Mia's face through the camera
exercise: What does "large language model" mean? | A huge copy of the patterns in how words are used | A big robot that speaks every language on Earth fluently | A tall model of a person made entirely out of words | A large book that lists every possible answer to everything
exercise: Sam's bot got the time wrong. Why might that happen? | It may have no clock and only know its old training text | It was too busy talking to look at a watch | It always lies about the time on purpose, to trick people | The time is a secret that bots are not told
exercise: Chloe asks whether AI dreams when it is switched off. What is true? | Nothing happens inside it when it is not running | It dreams at night about all the chats it had that day | It sleeps like a cat and wakes up rested in the morning | It keeps thinking quietly to itself all night long
exercise: Grandma calls the speaker "she" and thanks it. What is it really? | A machine with a chosen friendly voice | A lady who lives inside the little speaker | A recording of a real assistant on the phone | A neighbour who answers through the wires
exercise: Connor's bot and his friend's bot both pick 7. Why? | Lots of people pick 7, and bots copy common choices | Seven is the only number bots know | The two bots talked to each other first and agreed on it | Seven is the real answer to every question
exercise: Lily's dad says the bot learned from the internet. Lily asks, "Even the rude bits?" What is true? | It learned from good and bad text, and filters do not catch everything | The internet has no rude bits at all, so there's nothing to worry about | Bots only ever read children's books | Every rude word was removed by hand first
exercise: Jake asks the bot, "Are you a boy or a girl?" What is the honest answer? | Neither, it is a program with no body | A boy, because it has a deep voice | A girl, because it is kind | Both, depending on the day
exercise: Mum says AI is like a parrot. Big brother says that's unfair. Who is right? | Both partly: it mixes ideas in new ways, but learned it all from people | Mum, because it only ever repeats exact sentences that it once read somewhere online | Big brother, because it thinks just like a person | Neither, because AI never uses words people wrote
exercise: Maddie's horse picture appears in seconds. How was it made? | Picture AI learned from millions of described pictures and built a new one | A fast artist drew it on a tablet in a few seconds | The bot found an old photo of a horse | The bot cut and pasted pieces from all of Maddie's old photos of horses
exercise: Sam asks, "Is the bot just typing really fast?" What is happening? | Giant computers calculate the words; nobody is typing | A team of very fast typists works in shifts day and night to answer everyone | The bot types with tiny robot fingers | The words were all typed before Sam asked
exercise: How did a language model mainly learn? | By guessing missing and next words in huge amounts of text | By going to school with children every day for years and doing homework | By being told every answer by a teacher | By watching people through their cameras
exercise: How does a chatbot build its answer? | Piece by piece, picking a word that fits after the others | All at once, from a ready-made list of answers written by its makers | Letter by letter, from the end to the beginning | By choosing a random sentence from a book
exercise: Where do chatbots actually run? | On huge computers in buildings called data centres | Inside the small chip in your speaker only, and nowhere else | On the Moon, where it is cold | In the head of the person who built them
test: "my bot says it's 2023 lol, is it broken" What is likely going on? | Its training text ended long ago and it has no clock | It is broken inside and must be thrown away and replaced with a new one | It lives in a different time zone from you | It is joking because it is bored
test: What would happen if you left a chatbot app closed for a whole week? | Nothing; it would not miss you or think about you | It would feel lonely and sad, waiting for you to come back | It would plan what to say next time | It would dream about your last chat
test: "bots are just parrots, they copy and paste, end of" Is that fair? | Not quite; they mix patterns into new sentences, though they learned from people | Yes, every answer is copied word for word | No, bots invent everything with no help from people | Yes, they are actual birds inside a computer
test: Grandma says thank you to the speaker. Her grandson laughs. What is the kind truth? | Being polite is a good habit, but it is a device | She is right to, because it gets upset and sulks otherwise | It is silly to be polite to anything | She should apologise to it for laughing
test: In a cartoon a chatbot snores in its box at night. What is wrong with that? | A program that is not running does nothing at all | Chatbots only snore in the morning | Nothing, bots sleep eight hours a night | Bots dream but never snore
test: Ask ten bots for a number from 1 to 10. Which is most likely? | Many of them give 7, like many people do | Each gives a different number, fairly spread out | They all politely refuse to pick any number | They all say 10, because it is the biggest one
test: A bot gives an unkind answer about a group of people. What is the best way to think about it? | It may have picked up a bad pattern; tell a grown-up | Bots are always fair to everyone, so the answer must be right and true | It was written that way by a person on purpose | Bots cannot say unkind things, so it didn't happen
test: Your friend says the bot is "a she, called Alexa or whatever". What is closer to the truth? | A name and voice are labels chosen by its makers | It is a woman who works from home and answers questions all day | It is a girl robot hidden in a cupboard | It chose its own name when it was born
test: Why do words appear one after another when a bot answers? | It works out the answer in pieces and shows them as they come | It is a real person somewhere typing as fast as they possibly can for you | It is waiting for you to read each word | The internet is too slow to send a whole sentence at once
test: Which of these did a language model NOT need to learn? | A body to feel things with | Huge amounts of text to learn from | Powerful computers to run on | People to build and train it
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the term "large language model"? | A very extensive program that has acquired the patterns of written language | A physical model of the human organs of speech | A dictionary of exceptional size printed upon paper | A person of great stature who is fluent in many languages
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a conversational program may state the time incorrectly. | It may lack access to a clock, its knowledge being confined to text gathered before a certain date | It is required by regulation to conceal the correct time from its users | It deliberately misleads every user in respect of the time | It computes time solely from the position of the Moon
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a conversational program which has been switched off. | No activity whatsoever occurs within it until it is restarted | It continues to reflect upon prior conversations throughout the night | It enters a state of sleep accompanied by dreams | It experiences loneliness in the absence of its users
exam: It is given that numerous distinct programs, when requested to select a number between one and ten, select seven. Ascertain the most probable explanation. | Such programs tend to reproduce the most frequent human preference | The programs have conferred with one another in advance of the request | Seven is the only integer such programs are able to produce | The selection is governed by the date of the request
